**Read:** [Petrology — Stephen A. Nelson, Tulane EENS 2120](../sources/Petrology-Nelson-Tulane.lingua#r_1MNEKH4WW2GQRD4FZWNRD1GDPZ), the complete relevant volatile/fluid and magmatic notes; [Advanced Igneous Petrology — Timothy Grove, MIT 12.490](../sources/MIT-Advanced-Igneous-Petrology.lingua#r_3R0PJP97ZPE1RSGPPGY8WXXQZF), the full selected volatile-bearing system case; [HPx-eos and THERMOCALC — models, data, and interpretation](../sources/HPx-eos-and-THERMOCALC.lingua#r_0QYHHGNWRA8RQEM5MADWXDRQFJ), the complete model/reference conditions used for the study.

Volatile components influence melting, crystallization, density/rheology, fluid separation, transport, and alteration. Their solubility and partitioning depend on composition, temperature, pressure, species, and the adopted model. A bubble or hydrous mineral is evidence within a history, not a complete measurement of the original volatile budget.

Distinguish dissolved volatile, separate fluid, crystal-bound components, inclusions, and later contamination or weathering. An erupted glass or preserved inclusion may have undergone loss, diffusion, crystallization, decrepitation, or re-equilibration. Correcting it to an earlier state requires a model and uncertainty rather than a universal adjustment.

Fluid separation can transport elements and connect magmatic systems with hydrothermal mineralization. A temporal/spatial association between an intrusion and a deposit needs geochemical, structural, chronological, and mass-transfer evidence to establish the particular relationship.

**Work:** compare two volatile histories compatible with a documented sample, preserving analytical method and domain context. Reconstruct a mass account for dissolved, separate, and exported components under a simple model. Identify independent observations or experiments that could discriminate degassing, late alteration, and inherited inclusions.

Read the complete volatile/fluid and igneous notes and primary study used. Specialist volcanology, fluid-inclusion methods, and economic geology deepen their unique measurement and process questions; this shared petrological sequence keeps phase and material constraints explicit.

